## Introduction
To meet the goals of reducing carbon dioxide emissions and achieving carbon neutrality, clean and sustainable energy sources should be urgently employed as substitutes for fossil energy [1]. Hydrogen energy is one of the most promising candidates for sustainable energy sources owing to its high calorific value and abundant reserves [2-4]. Electrochemical water splitting $\mathrm{(2H_{2}O\rightarrow}$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{O}_{2})$ is an attractive method for converting intermittent energy (e.g., solar, wind, and geothermal energies) to storable hydrogen energy [5,6]. However, the practical use of water electrolysis is not feasible due to the sluggish reaction kinetics [7]. Among the reported catalysts, noble metals (e.g., Pt and Ru) have shown excellent hydrogen evolution reaction (HER） activity [8-12]. However, their commercial applications are restricted by their scarcity and cost. Therefore, the development of non-noble metal HER catalysts is necessary for the industrial application of water electrolysis.
First-row transition metals (TMs) (e.g., Fe, $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf\cal C}0,}\end{array}$ and Ni) as well as their alloys, sulfides, phosphides, and nitrides exhibit competitive HER activities [13-20]. The strategy of encapsulating TM-based catalysts in carbon shells has been frequently employed to improve the catalytic activity and stability of electrocatalysts [21,22]. The carbon shells not only inhibit the migration of the catalysts but also protect them from corrosion during the electrochemical process [23, 24]. In addition, the high porosity and large surface area of the carbon shell facilitate the exposure of the active centers and local accessibility of the electrolytes [25]. Furthermore, the single atoms bonded with the heteroatoms (especially N atoms) in carbon shells have been reported to modify the electronic structures and optimize the adsorption capacitytotheintermediate,whichisbeneficialfor electrochemical reactions [26-30]. However, the $\mathrm{Co-N}_{x}$ single atom (SA) catalysts have been rarely reported for efficient HER performance in alkaline solution due to the sluggish process of water adsorption on TM SA surface. Co-based nanoparticles (NPs) have received considerable attention as alkaline HER catalysts owing to their low Gibbs free energy toward HER [13,24]. However, the high energy barrier of water dissociation on the Co NPs surface limits its application [31,32]. Hitherto, the synergic effect between the NPs and SAs in NC materials has not been reported to improve the HER activity [33, 34].
Owing to the high porosity, large surface area, and ultrahigh density of exposed metal ions, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn considerable attention as sacrificial precursors for the synthesis of the TM/C composites [35,36]. The organic ligands can be converted to heteroatom-doped carbon material (HCM), and the TM ions can be reduced by carbon through the carbonization process. In addition, the isolating effect of the TM SAs to NPs can directly minimize the aggregation and enrich the active sites [1l]. Based on the above considerations, we suggested a simple thermal treatment for synthesis of Co NPs@SAs-NC $(\mathrm{NC}=\mathrm{N}$ -doped porous carbon) electrocatalyst featuring Co NPs and SAs-doped NC. The Co NPs@SAs-NC catalyst exhibited outstanding HER activity in both acidic and alkaline media, with extraordinary long-term durability. Both poor activities of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ NPs-NC and Co SAs-NC established the indispensability of the Co NPs and Co SAs. The results of electrochemical performances and density functional theory (DFT) calculations reveal that the coexistence of the Co NPs, Co SAs and N species is responsible for the outstanding HER catalytic activity.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of ZIF-67
## 2.2 Synthesis of Co NPs@SAs-NC
The ZIF-67 powder $(200~\mathrm{mg})$ was calcined in a tube furnace at $850~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ at a heating rate of $5~\mathrm{{^{\circ}C}{\cdot}\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ under an $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere via an isothermal process at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ The product was etched with 1 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature, followed by filtration and washing with water and methanol. The obtained products were named $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{SAs-NC}.$
## 2.3 Synthesis of Co NPs-NC, Co SAs-NC and NC
For comparison, Co NPs-NC was prepared by carbonizing ZIF-67 powder at $850~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ at a heating rate of $5~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere in a tube furnace without isothermal process. The Co SAs-NC was prepared via acid etching of $\mathrm{\odot{NPs@SAs-NC}}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution (1 M) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The NC was prepared by acid etching of Co NPs-NC in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution (1 M) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$
## 2.4 Preparation of working electrodes
A glassy carbon electrode (GCE, $5~\mathrm{mm}$ diameter) was polished with 1,000,300,and $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ alumina suspensions to obtain a mirror-like surface, and then cleaned ultrasonically in the presence of nitric acid $\mathrm{(HNO_{3})}$ 、1 M)，acetone $\mathrm{(C_{3}H_{6}O)}$ ，and deionized water. The catalyst ink was composed of a catalyst $(5\mathrm{mg})$ ,ethanol $(860~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ ，water $(100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ ,and Nafion solution $(40~\upmu\mathrm{L},~5~\mathrm{wt}.\%)$ which were mixed ultrasonicaly for $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ . To prepare the working electrode, the ink $(12~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was drop-casted onto the surface of the clean GCE and dried in air (catalyst loading: $0.306\mathrm{mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}.$ 。
## 2.5 Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical properties of all the catalysts were measured using a CHI 760e electrochemical workstation (CH Instruments Corp., Ltd.) using a three-electrode system at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ A carbon rod served as the counter electrode, while $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ and $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrodes were used as reference electrodes in the linear sweep voltammetry(LSV)and cyclic voltammetry(CV） tests, respectively. The electrolytes were KOH solution (1 M, $\mathrm{\pH=}$ 13.8), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution $(0.5~\mathrm{M},\$ $\mathrm{pH}=0.3\$ ） and phosphate-buffered saline (PBS） solution （ $[0.5\mathrm{~M},$ $\mathrm{pH}=7.0\$ ，and the electrolyte solution was bubbled by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to remove dissolved oxygen before tests. The LSV tests were performed at a scan rate of 5 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ to obtain the polarization curves, and the overpotentials were calculated according to the Nernst equation
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{E}}/\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{E}}0}+2.303R T/F\cdot\mathrm{pH}+E_{\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{E}}0/\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{E}}}^{0}(\mathrm{alkaline~solution})}\\ &{\qquad=E_{\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{E}}/\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{E}}0}+0.9122}\\ &{E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Ag}\mathrm{C}1}+2.303R T/F\cdot\mathrm{pH}+E_{\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Ag}\mathrm{C}1}^{0}(\mathrm{acid~solution})}\\ &{\qquad=E_{\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Ag}\mathrm{C}1}+0.2223}\\ &{E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Ag}\mathrm{C}1}+2.303R T/F\cdot\mathrm{pH}+E_{\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Ag}\mathrm{C}1}^{0}(\mathrm{neutral~solution})}\\ &{\qquad=E_{\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Ag}\mathrm{C}1}+0.6176}\end{array}
$$
Tafel slopes of the electrocatalysts were calculated by fitting the polarization curve by using the Tafel equation $(\eta=b\log j+a;$ where $b$ is the Tafel slope; $j$ is the current density; $\eta$ is the overpotential). Long-term stability was measured using the CV tests method over l,000 cycles at a scan rate of $100\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ between -100 and $300~\mathrm{{\mV}}$ (vs. RHE) and chronopotentiometry at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $24{\mathrm{~h}}.$ 、The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA）was analyzed by testing the double layer capacitances $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ .And the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values of catalysts were studied by CVs with varied scanning rates from 10 to $50\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ The regions of potential were set at $-0.1\mathrm{-}0\mathrm{V}$ for 1 M KOH, 0-0.1 V for 0.5M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $0{-}0.1\mathrm{V}$ for $0.5\mathrm{M}$ PBS because no obvious electrochemical features corresponding to the Faradic current were observed.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of catalysts
The well-defined nanostructured ZIF-67 was constructed according to a previous report [37]. Subsequently, as shown in Fig.l,the ZIF-67 powder was calcined in a tube furnace at $850^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ via an isothermal process at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the Co $\mathrm{NPs@SAs-NC}$ nanocatalyst. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curve (Fig. Sl in the ESM) exhibits a two-step weight loss over $100{-}1,000\quad^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Stage one $(<~480~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ represents  the evaporation of the physically adsorbed solvents, and stage two $(480-1,000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ constitutes the carbonization of the organic ligands. It should be noted that the initial isothermal process at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is important, in this process ZIF-67 would laggingly release heteroatoms to form the defect sites which can capture the Co SAs; then in higher temperature, partial cobalt atoms would aggregate into large Co NP [38,39]. Furthermore, the subsequent high-temperature carbonization process facilitates the tuning of conductivity and N doping of porous carbon materials [40]. For comparison,we prepared samples of ZIF-67 carbonized in one step at $850~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without an isothermal process. As shown in the Xray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Fig.S2 in the ESM), the diffraction peaks located at $44.2^{\circ}$ and $51.5^{\circ}$ are consistent with Co( ${\mathrm{PDF}}\#15.$ 0806), demonstrating the formation of Co NPs after calcination of ZIF-67 at $850~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ 、In addition, the diffraction peak observed at $26.1^{\circ}$ is originated from the hexagonal graphitic structure of graphene carbon. In the Raman spectrum (Fig.S3 in the ESM), the unambiguous peaks at 1,355 and $1{,}580\ c m^{-1}$ correspond to the disordered $\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ carbon (d-band) and graphite $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon (G-band), respectively. The intensity ratio of the D and G bands $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ of Co NPs@SAs-NC is lower than that of Co NPs-NC. The XRD and Raman results show that Co NPs@SAs-NC has a higher graphitization degree which can improve the electrical conductivity and electrochemical catalytic activity of the catalysts.
The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface areas and porosities of the as-synthesized samples were analyzed by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption experiments at 77 K. According to Fig. S4 in the ESM, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm of ZIF-67 is type I, while those of $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs.NC}$ and Co NPs-NC are the combination of type I and type IV, reflecting the alteration of pore structure after calcination. The isotherms exhibit a marked increase in the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake at a relatively low pressure $(P/P_{0}<0.01)$ ， which indicates the presence of numerous micropores in samples. The BET surface areas of $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs.NC}$ and Co NPs-NC are 525.1 and $483.8~\mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ 、 respectively. Furthermore, the pore volumes of Co NPs@SAs-NC and Co NPs-NC are calculated to 0.723 and $0.698\ \mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ 、respectively. In addition, the pore size distribution curves obtained by the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method (Fig. S5 in the ESM) show a hierarchical porous structure that can enhance the surface -to -volume ratio and optimize the diffusion process to promote the HER reaction [41]. The inductively  coupled  plasma-optical  emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) result shows that the $\mathrm{Co}$ contents of $\mathrm{Co}$ $\mathrm{NPs@SAs-NC}$ and Co NPs-NC are $19.1~\mathrm{{\mt}}.\%$ and $22.2\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ respectively.
The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of ZIF-67 indicate a regular dodecahedral morphology with a smooth surface ( $250~\mathrm{nm}$ in size), and those of ( $\mathrm{\Sigma_{0}\ N P s@S A s{\mathrm{-}}N C}$ shows a wrinkled stereoscopic morphology ( $220\mathrm{nm}$ in size) (Figs. 2(a) and $2(\mathrm{b})_{.}^{\cdot}$ . Therefore, the spatial structure of Co NPs@SAs-NC did not collapse, which facilitated the diffusion of water in the nanocatalyst. In addition, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained for morphological analysis. As observed in Fig.2(c), the sphere-like Co NPs (black dots) are uniformly scattered in the porous NC (gray substrate) derived from ZIF-67. The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Fig. 2(d)) shows that the Co NPs are completely encapsulated in the NC shell, indicating a close contact between Co lattice and Co SAs-doped NC surface. The average thickness of NC shell was measured to $2.3~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S6 in the ESM), which can prevent the aggregation of Co particles and won't hinder ion migration in the electrolyte [42]. Meanwhile, the fringe spacings were measured to be 0.21 and $0.36\ \mathrm{nm}$ 。corresponding to the $\mathrm{Co}(111)$ and C(002) lattice planes, respectively [23,43]. This result is consistent with the XRD peaks mentioned above.Besides, the atomic distribution of Co SAs in the carbon matrix can be observed in Figs. 2(e) and 2(f). Furthermore, the energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental  mapping images (Fig. $2(\mathrm{g)}$ ）illustrate a uniform distribution of Co, $\mathrm{C},$ and N, which is evidence of N doping. A a comparison, the Co NPs-NC is more distorted than $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{SAs}.$ NC shown in the SEM image (Fig.S7(a) in the ESM), but it still maintains the three-dimensional (3D) framework structures. TEM analyses of the Co NPs-NC show an incompact contact between the Co NPs and the NC shell (Figs. S7(b) and S7(c) in the ESM), and the EDS elemental mapping images show that most of the Co element exists as isolated $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ particles (Fig. S7(d) in the ESM). The above TEM analyses prove that a proper isothermal process can promote the generation of single atoms and heterojunction between Co NPs and NC. The obtained Co SAs-doped NC shell can protect Co NPs from erosion during the electrochemical process, and Co NPs can be locked in the carbon matrix due to the strong interaction between Co SAs and Co NPs [ll]. More importantly, the felicitous synergistic interaction between the Co NPs and Co SAs-doped NC shell may play a key role in improving the HER performance.
## 3.2 Electrocatalytic performance
The HER activity of the Co NPs@SAs-NC catalyst in an alkaline solution (1 M KOH） was investigated using a three-electrode system, and compared with those of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C},$ Co NPsNC, and Co SAs-NC. The Co SAs-NC and NC were fabricated by etching Co NPs@SAs-NC and Co NPs-NC with 1M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ under hydrothermal conditions to eliminate Co NPs, respectively. As shown in Fig.S1l in the ESM, the Co NPs in the carbon matrix are completely removed. Besides, there is no ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{\mathrm{0}}}}$ peak in XRD pattern and XPS spectra (Fig.Sl2 in the ESM). To prepare the working electrode, all the catalysts (in powder form) were dispersed in a mixed ethanol/water/Nafion solution followed by drop-casting onto the GCE surface. As shown in Fig.4(a), the $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst exhibits the highest HER activity with an overpotential (expressed henceforth as $\eta_{10}\mathrm{,}$ of $46~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Among the prepared catalysts, Co NPs@SAs-NC exhibits a remarkable catalytic performance toward HER with an $\eta_{10}$ value of $110~\mathrm{mV}_{:}$ which is higher than those of most TM-based electrocatalysts in an alkaline solution (Table S2 in the ESM).However, the catalytic activities of Co NPs-NC and Co SAs-NC decrease significantly (the values of $\eta_{10}$ are 193 and $526\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mV}}_{:}$ 、 respectively), indicating Co NPs and SAs have the synergistic effects on HER reaction under the alkaline condition. To further understand the HER mechanism, the Tafel slope was obtained using the Tafel equation $(\eta=\mathrm{blog}j+\mathsf{a})$ deduced from the polarization curve. The Tafel slope of $20\%\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ is $31\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ (Fig.4(b)),which is close to previously reported values [49,50]. Meanwhile, the Tafel slope of C $\mathrm{~o~NPs@SAs–NC}$ 1 $(55~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ is smaller than those of Co NPs-NC $(64\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ ，Co SAs-NC $(169\mathrm{~mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1},$ ), suggesting that the electrocatalytic kinetic of Co $\mathrm{NPs@SAs-NC}$ was more favorable for HER. Additionally,the durability of Co NPs@SAs-NC was tested using CV and longterm chronopotentiometry. As shown in Fig. 4(c), the polarization curve after $1{,}000~\mathrm{CV}$ sweeps is like the initial one, and the $\nu{-}t$ curve remains stable for $24\ \mathrm{h},$ indicating that the SAs-doped NC shell protected the Co NPs from erosion during the electrochemical process. To further reveal the effect of $\mathrm{Co-N}_{x}$ sites in Co NPs@SAs-NC, we used phosphate to block the N sites by forming pyridinium (N-H) structure during the LSV process [51,52]. As expected, $\mathrm{\odot\NPs@SAs{\mathrm{-}}N C}$ exhibits poor catalytic activities with $\eta_{10}$ values of 148 and $215\mathrm{mV}$ in 0.5 and 5 M $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ solutions, respectively (Fig. Sl3 in the ESM), demonstrating the critical role of the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ site in the HER process. The doublelayer capacitances $\mathrm{(}C_{\mathrm{dl}}\mathrm{)}$ can be obtained by the CV tests with scanning rates from 10 to $50\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ .The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of the( $\mathrm{\odot_{NPs@SAs}}$ NC is higher than those of Co-NPs-NC and Co-SAs-NC (Fig. S14 in the ESM), indicating that Co NPs@SAs-NC has the largest active area in three Co-based catalysts.
The HER activity of Co NPs@SAs-NC catalyst in an acidic solution $(0.5~\mathrm{~M~}~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ ）was also performed. As shown in Fig.4(d), the commercial $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ exhibits the best actives with $41~\mathrm{\mV}$ overpotential at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ current density and $29\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}d e c^{-1}}$ of Tafel slope (Fig.4(e)). The Co $\mathrm{NPs@SAs-NC}$ shows a better performance with $47~\mathrm{\mV}$ overpotential  at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ than Co NPs-NC $(\eta_{10}=132~\mathrm{mV})$ and Co SAs-NC $\mathrm{(\eta_{10}=67~m V)}$ . The HER activity of $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs.NC}$ is higher than those of most noble-free TM electrocatalysts in the acidic solutions (Table S3 in the ESM). The Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Co}\ N P s@\mathrm{SAs}.$ NC was calculated to $42\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ 。compared with Co NPs-NC $(70\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ ）and Co SAs-NC $(54\mathrm{~mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ . The Tafel slope result indicates that the catalytic activities of $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs.NC}$ toward HER follow the Volmer--Heyrovsky mechanism in $0.5\mathrm{M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ [53]. After l,000 cycles of long-term CV test, there is a negligible reduction of current density compared with the first cycle (Fig.4(f). The double-layer capacitances $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ data of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ $\mathrm{NPs@SAs-NC}$ in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ are displayed in Fig.S15 in the ESM, and the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value of $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs.NC}$ is the highest in the three Co-based nanocatalysts under the acidic condition.
We also evaluated the HER performances of Co NPs@SAs-NC catalyst in neutral electrolytes ( $\mathrm{~\mathit~{~0.5~M~}~}$ PBS), and the result is shown in Figs. 4(g)-4(i). The overpotentials at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ current density of $\mathrm{Pt/C},$ Co NPs@SAs-NC, Co NPs-NC and Co SAs-NC are 128,171,316 and $707~\mathrm{mV}$ respectively (Fig.4(f). The Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ was calculated to $52\quad\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ whichis corresponding to the reported results [54]; the $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs.NC}$ (77 mV·dec-) shows a better performance than Co NPs-NC $(114\mathrm{~mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1},$ ）and Co SAs-NC $(194~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ ）(Fig.4(f)). The stability of ( ${\tt C o}\ {\tt N P s@S A s-N C}$ in neutral media was evaluated by 100 cycles of long-term CV test. Compare with the first cycle there is a negligible reduction of current density after $1{,}000\ \mathrm{CV}$ cycles (Fig.4(f)). The double-layer capacitances $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ results of Co $\mathrm{NPs@SAs-NC}$ in $0.5\mathrm{M}$ PBS are displayed in Fig. Sl6 in the ESM and the $C_{\mathrm{d}}$ value was calculated to $25.32~\mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ which is higher than those of Co NPs-NC $(8.73~\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})$ ）and Co SAs-NC $(7.51\mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}}.$ 。
The characterization of Co NPs@SAs-NC after HER test demonstrates well-matched phase and valence states from XRD (Fig.Sl7(a) in the ESM) and XPS resultd (Fig.S17(b)-S17(d) in the ESM) and the morphology maintains well from TEM images (Fig. S18 in the ESM), further suggesting the superb stability.
In general, the catalytic performances of Co NPs@SAs-NC toward HER in acidic, alkaline, and neutral media are the best among three Co-based catalysts, revealing the synergy between the Co NPs and Co SAs-doped NC. The sample NC without Co species shows a poor catalytic performance (Fig. 4), implying that the N-doped carbon matrix has few contributions to catalytic activity.
## 3.3 DFT calculations
To investigate the synergy between the Co NPs and Co SAs-doped NC,we employed DFT calculations to obtain the electronic structures and energy changes of Co NPs-NC, Co SAs-NC and Co NPs@SAs-NC. As shown in Fig.5(a) and Figs. S19-S21 in the ESM,the Co $\mathrm{NPs@SAs}$ heterointerface model was constructed using $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ single-layered graphene and the $\mathrm{Co}(111)$ plane because the peak intensity of $\mathrm{Co}(111)$ is higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}(200)$ in the XRD pattern, and the lattice fringe of $\mathrm{Co}(111)$ is observed in the HRTEM image. The $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ model was composed by substituting four pyridinic N atoms bonded with one $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ atom for C atoms in graphene model. The charge density difference images show strong electron transfer from the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ superlattice to Co single atom (Fig. 5(b)), and the quantity of transferred electron is $0.1\mathrm{~e~}^{-}$ via Bader charge analysis (Table S4 in the ESM). Besides, the partial density of states $(\mathrm{pDOS})$ data show that the DOS of Co single atoms near the Fermi level is mainly originated from the d state. Meanwhile, the calculated d-band centers of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ single atom relative to the Fermi level are $-0.87~\mathrm{eV}$ (Co NPs@SAs-NC) and $-1.16\mathrm{eV}$ (Co SAs-NC) (Fig.5(c), respectively. These results indicate that the Co single atom of Co NPs@SAs-NC has a stronger electronic attraction, thereby increasing the charge density of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ single atoms and balancing the adsorption and desorption of H atoms [55]. The HER activity of a catalyst in the acidic solution is typically described by the Gibbs free energy of the intermediate H adsorption $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}})$ , and a low value of $|\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}}|$ is desirable. The calculation results indicate that the H atoms are adsorbed in the top position of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ single atom in the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ NPs@SAs-NC and Co SAs-NC, whereas adsorbed in the hollow position of Co atoms in the Co NPs-NC (Fig.S22 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 5(d), the $\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}}$ corresponding to the Cc $)\mathrm{NPs@SAs}$ is approximately $0.12\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ which is much closer to O than those of Co SAs( $\mathrm{(0.35~eV)}$ and Co NPs $(-0.38\ \mathrm{eV})$ ，indicating that the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ $\mathrm{NPs@SAs}$ executed thermo-neutral adsorption of protons to improve the HER activity. Considering the favorable pathways on three models under the alkaline condition (Figs. S23-S25 in the ESM), the Co NPs@SAs model is more thermodynamically favorable than Co NPs and Co SAs (Fig. 5(e)). Generally, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecular can be adsorbed appropriately via ${\mathrm{Co-H}}$ bond in Co SAs and $\mathrm{Co\NPs@SAs,}$ and then directly dissociated, while the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecular is adsorbed via $\mathrm{Co-O}$ bond in Co NPs. In addition, the adsorption energy of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ on Co NPs $(-1.05\ \mathrm{eV})$ is much lower than those on Co SAs $(-0.24\ \mathrm{eV})$ , indicating that the Co nanoparticles may enrich and activate water molecules and promote the water splitting process.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, Co NPs and SAs encapsulated in N-doped porous carbon（ $\mathrm{\DeltaCo\NPs@SAs{\mathrm{-}}N C},$ ）were successfully synthesized via a facile carbonization process using ZIF-67 as a sacrificial precursor. The segmented calcination method contributes to the co-existence and homogeneous distribution of Co NPs and SAs. The Co NPs@SAs-NC exhibits prominent electrocatalytic activities toward HER with an overpotential of $110~\mathrm{mV}$ in the alkaline solution, $47\mathrm{mV}$ in the acidic solution and $171\ \mathrm{mV}$ in the neutral solution to reach a current density of $10~\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ 、 Because of the protection of the carbon shell, it still retains its stability after an uninterrupted chronopotentiometry test is performed for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ According to the experiment tests and DFT calculations, the HER performance is improved by the synergistic effect of Co NPs and $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ sites. This work provides an insight into the synergistic effect between the TM NPs and SAs-doped carbon materials and offers a viable synthetic scheme to obtain $\mathrm{NPs@SAs}$ catalysts.
